WEBVTT

00:11.382 --> 00:16.927
In 1755, a Virginia mother slipped out of a
Shawnee salt camp in Kentucky,

00:16.987 --> 00:18.245
and set out for home.

00:18.452 --> 00:20.597
The river... was her only map.

00:21.716 --> 00:23.364
Draper's Meadow, Virginia.

00:23.558 --> 00:27.275
Mary Draper Ingles was twenty-three,
with her husband Will,

00:27.348 --> 00:28.875
and two small sons.

00:29.984 --> 00:31.350
The land was not empty.

00:31.557 --> 00:34.387
The Shawnee had been pushed
west for generations.

00:34.594 --> 00:38.285
And now, Britain and France were at
war for the Ohio Valley.

00:39.545 --> 00:43.430
<i>We had been pushed from river to
river for a hundred years.</i>

00:43.506 --> 00:46.816
<i>When the British army fell on
the Monongahela,</i>

00:46.856 --> 00:48.853
<i>many of us chose to fight.</i>

00:49.891 --> 00:52.923
On July 30th, 1755...

00:53.130 --> 00:55.948
harvest day... a Shawnee war party struck.

00:57.021 --> 00:58.494
Mary's mother was killed.

00:58.687 --> 01:02.889
Mary, her two boys, and her wounded
sister-in-law were taken.

01:03.096 --> 01:04.179
Will... escaped.

01:08.127 --> 01:11.272
For a month, the captives were
marched down the rivers,

01:11.312 --> 01:12.449
to the Ohio.

01:17.114 --> 01:20.170
<i>I could have slipped away more than once.</i>

01:20.338 --> 01:22.376
<i>I would not leave my boys.</i>

01:23.511 --> 01:27.390
At the Shawnee town on the Scioto, her
boys were taken from her,

01:27.557 --> 01:29.353
and given to different families.

01:30.592 --> 01:34.580
<i>A captive child could fill the place
of someone we had lost.</i>

01:34.696 --> 01:36.775
<i>To us, he was family.</i>

01:37.782 --> 01:41.486
That fall, she was taken to Big
Bone Lick, Kentucky...

01:41.572 --> 01:44.268
to boil salt among mastodon bones.

01:45.510 --> 01:49.214
<i>My boys were gone. I knew
how far home was.</i>

01:49.345 --> 01:55.173
<i>Better to try for it, or die in the woods,
than live out my days a captive.</i>

01:56.487 --> 02:02.301
In October, she and an older captive, known
only as “the old Dutch woman,”

02:02.481 --> 02:04.102
went out to gather grapes...

02:04.161 --> 02:05.487
and never came back.

02:06.572 --> 02:10.343
They followed the Ohio upstream,
wading creek after creek,

02:10.402 --> 02:12.427
living on walnuts and pawpaws.

02:13.503 --> 02:17.637
Then came the snow. Starving, the
older woman turned on Mary...

02:17.737 --> 02:19.344
and twice tried to kill her.

02:20.622 --> 02:22.506
<i>She talked me into leaving.</i>

02:22.654 --> 02:27.435
<i>Then came the snow and the hunger, and I
blamed her for every step.</i>

02:28.460 --> 02:32.796
Mary broke free. And at night, she
crossed the river in a canoe,

02:32.949 --> 02:35.027
paddling with a splintered slab.

02:36.121 --> 02:41.161
Around December 1st, frostbitten and alone,
she called out at a neighbor's

02:41.201 --> 02:46.478
cornfield. Adam Harman knew it at once:
“certainly Mary Ingles voice.”

02:56.257 --> 02:59.988
Forty-two days. Perhaps five or
six hundred miles.

03:00.195 --> 03:02.233
The old Dutch woman survived too.

03:03.578 --> 03:08.743
<i>John Draper and I had gone to the
Cherokee to ransom our families.</i>

03:08.932 --> 03:12.235
<i>We were seven miles off the
night she came in.</i>

03:13.282 --> 03:15.548
Mary and Will founded Ingles Ferry.

03:15.755 --> 03:19.271
Thomas came home at seventeen,
speaking only Shawnee.

03:19.464 --> 03:20.870
George never returned.

03:21.064 --> 03:24.835
Raids and reprisals went on for
years, on both sides.

03:25.892 --> 03:28.911
Legend grew around her, but
the core is true.

03:29.118 --> 03:32.957
She died in 1815. She had
followed the river home.
